            <ArticleTitle>Continuous Aspiration Mechanical Thrombectomy for the Management of Submassive Pulmonary Embolism: A Single-Center Experience.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1348-1352</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1051-0443(17)30602-4</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jvir.2017.06.025</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The Indigo Mechanical Thrombectomy System (Penumbra, Inc, Alameda, California) was used to treat 6 patients with submassive pulmonary embolism (PE) and a contraindication to thrombolysis. Systolic pulmonary artery pressure (58.2 mm Hg vs 43.0 mm Hg, P &lt; .05), right ventricular/left ventricular ratio (1.7 vs 1.1, P &lt; .05), Miller index (15.0 vs 9.8, P &lt; 0.01), and CT obstructive index (60.4% vs 47.0%, P &lt; .01) were significantly reduced after mechanical thrombectomy. There were no procedural or periprocedural complications. Continuous aspiration mechanical thrombectomy is a feasible and promising technique for management of submassive PE to decrease thrombus burden and reduce right heart strain.</AbstractText>
